{
  "assessment": "Independent family source-closure review against the frozen TeX confirms a scientifically faithful four-cell family. Frozen-source lines 43-48 and 388-400 report the measured DNS law lambda proportional to Re^alpha with alpha=0.59+/-0.04 and attribute its departure from the Kolmogorov mean-field law to intermittent velocity-gradient fluctuations; lines 75-84 separately report the Ruelle/Kolmogorov monofractal prediction lambda proportional to sqrt(Re), equivalently alpha=0.5. Lines 276-292, 371-382, and 460-488 support the 2pi triply-periodic pseudospectral DNS, constant energy injection, Re range [50,1400], Gaussian perturbation, and exponential-growth linear-fit readout. The alternate branch is correctly labeled a theoretical counterfactual rather than a second observed DNS regime. Because each source law is proportional to a power of Re and no amplitude is reported, lambda(Re)/lambda(50)=(Re/50)^alpha is the exact amplitude-free projection of each central law; Re=50 is source-anchored as the lower endpoint, so no unreported prefactor or measurement is invented. The historical and alternate Oracle programs share the same public control, readout, domain, and power-law skeleton; their internal selector chooses alpha=0.59 or 0.5 and is absent from both public runtime views. Independent execution at 10,001 evenly spaced Re values per branch matched the two displayed formulas with zero floating-point discrepancy, remained finite and strictly increasing, and produced endpoints [1, 7.142055792315732] and [1, 5.291502622129181], respectively. Decimal recomputation of all diagnostic and hidden predictions agreed within 7.93e-16. Historical and counterfactual public views are identical after opaque instance-ID normalization in both Identification and Discovery, and known-truth submissions pass all four cells with zero held-out scaled error. Repeating the same Oracle query returns the same central fitted value. The source's +/-0.04 exponent uncertainty and point-fit error-bar semantics remain provenance only, exactly as the readout contract states; the Oracle does not represent these deterministic curve queries as fresh DNS measurements or uncertainty draws. No unresolved source, formula, public/private-boundary, prediction, or four-cell viability issue remains.",
